WEBVTT

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:07.730
Hi, this is Dr. Jelena Trajkovic from the CECS Department at Cal State Long Beach. The topic

00:00:07.730 --> 00:00:16.080
of this video is how to create an instance of the module using Verilog. What we do first

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:23.580
is we write a code for module. In this code, we can identify ports of the module, input

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:29.560
and output, and body of the module. But in order to use it, we need to create an instance

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:36.600
of a module. We have already seen in lab 1 that we created an instance of the module inside of test

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:46.440
batch where we use this module instance basically to create to test the functionality of the module

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:54.040
itself. Also we can use it in structural or hierarchical design inside of the other module

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:59.420
and we will talk about this later and use it in future labs.

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:05.660
So let us look into test bench itself. Test bench is a wrapper

00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:10.380
around the module and in test bench we need to create

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:14.300
for each input port of the module we need to

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:18.700
create a variable of type of register. Bear in mind

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:23.820
that a number of bits that this variable has

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:30.460
has to be the same as number of bits that input port or output port has

00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:44.220
in the module that we want to test. Example in 0 has 3 bits 2 to 0 and also carry tb has just one bit

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:49.220
and carry has one with two as well.

00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:54.220
So for inputs, we need to create variables of type of register.

00:01:54.220 --> 00:02:02.220
For outputs, we need to create variables of type of wires.

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:05.220
It is important that the inputs are type of register,

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:09.220
because register is variable that can hold the value.

00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:18.340
After we have been creating the variables here in the variable

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:20.860
declaration part of the test bench,

00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:24.580
we have to create the instance of the module.

00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:26.700
We have two options to do that.

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:31.660
One is connecting by name and the other one is connecting by order list.

00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:36.000
The option that is preferred for this course is connecting by name,

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:39.060
and it is shown in here.

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:42.780
As you can see, there is the name of the module,

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:44.900
then there is the name of instance,

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:53.020
and then there is dot followed by name of the port within module,

00:02:53.020 --> 00:02:54.700
and in parentheses,

00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:59.340
there is a name of the variable that is from the test bench.

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:06.340
We can repeat that for each input or output port of the module.

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:14.680
It is curious to notice that, in this connection by name scheme, the order of appearance of

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:22.560
port is not important because they are basically name of the port is connected to name of the

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:31.280
variable where basically the port is connected to variable here and there is no ambiguity.

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:39.800
This is more work in order to type in all the names of the ports and names of the variable,

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:42.320
but it is much more readable.

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:48.040
The preferred way of doing this in the course would be the previous one, where the order

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:50.400
and the names are all listed.

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:57.200
However, there is another way of doing it, and this is connected by order list, where

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:05.360
name of the ports of ADD underscore BEH module are omitted, but then the order needs to be

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:12.880
exactly the same as the order of appearance of ports when modules declared.

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:22.240
This is shorter, but way more error prone method of instantiating modules.

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:32.600
As I said, our preferred method will be using the syntax shown in the first example, but

00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:36.640
for the sake of completion, I wanted to show you and offer you all the options.

00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:37.440
Thanks for watching.

